## Notes on enabling/disabling blocking

The above code stores the toggle state, but the static DNR rules are always active if enabled in the manifest. To truly enable/disable blocking, you should use **dynamic rules** instead of only static rules.

Here’s a better version of `background.js` that toggles rules dynamically:

---

## Improved `background.js` with dynamic rules

```js
const RULE_IDS = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6];

chrome.runtime.onInstalled.addListener(() => {
  chrome.storage.local.get(["enabled"], async (result) => {
    const enabled = result.enabled !== false;
    await setBlocking(enabled);
  });
});

chrome.storage.onChanged.addListener((changes, area) => {
  if (area === "local" && changes.enabled) {
    setBlocking(changes.enabled.newValue !== false);
  }
});

async function setBlocking(enabled) {
  if (enabled) {
    const rules = await fetch(chrome.runtime.getURL("rules.json")).then((r) => r.json());

    await chrome.declarativeNetRequest.updateDynamicRules({
      removeRuleIds: RULE_IDS,
      addRules: rules
    });
  } else {
    await chrome.declarativeNetRequest.updateDynamicRules({
      removeRuleIds: RULE_IDS
    });
  }
}
```

