## 3) `background.js`

This service worker initializes state and can later manage user preferences or dynamic rules.

```js
chrome.runtime.onInstalled.addListener(async () => {
  const defaults = {
    enabled: true,
    blockedCount: 0
  };

  const current = await chrome.storage.local.get(Object.keys(defaults));
  const toSet = {};

  for (const [key, value] of Object.entries(defaults)) {
    if (current[key] === undefined) toSet[key] = value;
  }

  if (Object.keys(toSet).length > 0) {
    await chrome.storage.local.set(toSet);
  }
});

chrome.declarativeNetRequest.onRuleMatchedDebug?.addListener((info) => {
  chrome.storage.local.get({ blockedCount: 0 }).then(({ blockedCount }) => {
    chrome.storage.local.set({ blockedCount: blockedCount + 1 });
  });
});
```

> Note: `onRuleMatchedDebug` may require additional debugging permissions in some environments and is not intended for production analytics. If unavailable, you can remove it and track counts in another way later.

