Below is a minimal working embedded-style sensor-processing pipeline for anomaly detection from accelerometer data.

It is intentionally simple:
- reads 3-axis accelerometer samples
- computes magnitude
- maintains a rolling baseline
- flags anomalies when the current magnitude deviates too much from the baseline

## C example

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <math.h>
#include <stdbool.h>

#define WINDOW_SIZE 16
#define ANOMALY_THRESHOLD 2.5f   // tune this for your sensor/noise level

typedef struct {
    float x, y, z;
} AccelSample;

typedef struct {
    float buffer[WINDOW_SIZE];
    int index;
    int count;
    float sum;
} RollingWindow;

static void window_init(RollingWindow *w) {
    w->index = 0;
    w->count = 0;
    w->sum = 0.0f;
    for (int i = 0; i < WINDOW_SIZE; i++) {
        w->buffer[i] = 0.0f;
    }
}

static void window_add(RollingWindow *w, float value) {
    if (w->count < WINDOW_SIZE) {
        w->buffer[w->index] = value;
        w->sum += value;
        w->count++;
    } else {
        w->sum -= w->buffer[w->index];
        w->buffer[w->index] = value;
        w->sum += value;
    }
    w->index = (w->index + 1) % WINDOW_SIZE;
}

static float window_mean(const RollingWindow *w) {
    if (w->count == 0) return 0.0f;
    return w->sum / (float)w->count;
}

static float accel_magnitude(AccelSample s) {
    return sqrtf(s.x * s.x + s.y * s.y + s.z * s.z);
}

static bool is_anomaly(float current_mag, float baseline_mean) {
    return fabsf(current_mag - baseline_mean) > ANOMALY_THRESHOLD;
}

int main(void) {
    RollingWindow baseline;
    window_init(&baseline);

    // Example stream of accelerometer samples
    AccelSample samples[] = {
        {0.01f, 0.02f, 1.00f},
        {0.00f, 0.01f, 0.99f},
        {0.02f, 0.01f, 1.01f},
        {0.01f, 0.00f, 1.00f},
        {0.03f, 0.02f, 0.98f},
        {2.50f, 1.80f, 3.20f}, // anomaly
        {0.01f, 0.02f, 1.00f},
    };

    int sample_count = sizeof(samples) / sizeof(samples[0]);

    for (int i = 0; i < sample_count; i++) {
        float mag = accel_magnitude(samples[i]);
        float mean = window_mean(&baseline);

        bool anomaly = false;
        if (baseline.count >= 4) { // wait until baseline is somewhat stable
            anomaly = is_anomaly(mag, mean);
        }

        printf("Sample %d: mag=%.3f baseline=%.3f %s\n",
               i, mag, mean, anomaly ? "ANOMALY" : "OK");

        window_add(&baseline, mag);
    }

    return 0;
}
```

## How it works
1. **Magnitude calculation**
   - Converts 3-axis accelerometer data into one scalar:
   - `sqrt(x² + y² + z²)`

2. **Rolling baseline**
   - Keeps the last `WINDOW_SIZE` magnitudes
   - Uses their mean as a simple normal behavior estimate

3. **Anomaly detection**
   - If the current magnitude differs from the baseline mean by more than `ANOMALY_THRESHOLD`, it is flagged

## Embedded-friendly notes
- For microcontrollers without an FPU, replace `sqrtf` with an approximation or compare squared magnitudes.
- You can make it more robust by using:
  - rolling standard deviation
  - median filter
  - high-pass filtering
  - a small ML model or one-class classifier

