## Notes

### Why fixed timestep?
A fixed timestep is the standard way to keep physics stable and deterministic across varying frame rates.

- Render may run at 30/60/144+ FPS
- Physics runs at a constant rate, e.g. 60 or 120 Hz
- The accumulator ensures you simulate the correct amount of time

### What this code does not yet include
This is a starter implementation. A production rigid-body system would also need:

- full 3D collision shapes
- broadphase collision detection
- narrowphase contact generation
- multiple contact points
- constraint solver / iterative impulse solver
- sleeping/activation
- continuous collision detection
- proper world-space inertia tensor transforms
- stable friction and stacking

